{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788304678,"nonce":"/sAJ95JLiB/b++6VMLU3N0cvjzI=","message":"RULED BY EVAN AND CLOSED, 2026-09-01. Bullet 1 is REWORDED to the form that shipped. This issue has carried an unsatisfied bullet across four sessions on purpose, so the closing argument is recorded rather than assumed.\n\n## The bullet as it stood\n\n\u003e A verb call carrying an unrecognised argument key is refused with `bad-args` naming the key.\n\n## The bullet as it now reads\n\n\u003e A verb call carrying an unrecognised argument key is never SILENT: the key is named in the envelope's `ignored_args` alongside the keys the call actually read, and in the journal row. Where the verb's default MUTATES, the call is REFUSED with `bad-args` before any step runs.\n\n## Why the general refusal is not being built, with the number\n\nMeasured, not asserted: **73 keys across 26 verbs** are read only on some code paths while the verb legitimately succeeds. Four shapes, each exercised on purpose by a shipped suite:\n\n- `zone {op:\"add\", plant, label, dry_run:true}` — the preflight skips the only reader of `plant`\n- `dev:spawn-thing {stockpile, pos}` — `pos` goes unread when storage accepts\n- `queue` after the per-pawn gates in twelve verbs, so `wear {queue:true}` refused by its gate leaves `queue` unread — and `accept/4087644-order-honesty.py` is a suite ABOUT those refusals\n- `bill-add` answering `NotAWorkTable` before `ValidateBillArgs` reads its twenty levers\n\nPlus `rwa/selftest.sh`'s `ping --n 500 --flag --word Normal`, a deliberate CLI-cast test.\n\nRefusing those refuses CORRECT calls, mid-run, on a live bench, during a ten-day unattended run. The failure mode of the literal bullet is strictly worse than the defect it was written against.\n\n## What is actually fixed, and it is the thing the title names\n\nThe title is \"an unknown argument name is silently ignored and falls back to a default, so a wrong arg reports success.\" Three clauses, and the load-bearing ones are **silently** and **reports success**.\n\n- **Silence is gone for all 128 verbs**, with no declaration anywhere. `VerbArgs` keeps a read log — every accessor funnels through one `Look(key)` and `supplied − queried` is the unknown-argument set — so it is derived from the code rather than maintained beside it. The 22 forwarding handlers and five runtime-built dicts stopped being special cases because the log follows the OBJECT.\n- **The destructive instance is refused before it acts.** `journal-selftest {kind:\"save\"}` — the call that downed all three colonists and started a berserk while returning `ok:true` — now returns `bad-args`, names `kind`, names all seventeen arguments the verb accepts, and says nothing was mutated. Proven on bench `20260901T212651` with the colonist roster read immediately before and after: **identical, three up, none downed, none in a mental state.**\n- **The inconsistency comment #7 drew out is gone.** A typo'd VALUE and a typo'd KEY both produce a message now, rather than a well-mannered refusal and silent damage.\n\n## The one place the general property still bites, said plainly\n\nA stray key on a verb whose default is harmless still SUCCEEDS. `digest --wibble 3` returns `ok:true` and publishes `ignored_args`. A caller that does not read `ignored_args` learns nothing — which is the same shape as M1's advance results carrying `journal_seq` that nobody read, and `722c951` had to add a refusal to fix that. If that pattern recurs here, the answer is a targeted refusal on the specific verb, not a global whitelist, and this comment is the evidence for why.\n\n## Not closed silently\n\nThe measurement in comment #3, the honest \"bullet 1 is NOT met\" in comments #5 and #6, and the bench pass in comment #8 are why this issue was useful for four sessions. Closing it against the reworded bullet, not against the original — the original was a good ask and the cost of satisfying it is now a known number rather than a guess.\n\nTwo things it produced that outlast it: `VerbArgs.NearMiss`, and `pos_source` in both the envelope and the journal row. And a live catch nobody planned — during session 21 the orchestrator passed `area --op create --label` where the verb reads `name`, and `ignored_args` named the key, listed the four keys the verb did read, and handed over the journal seq range. That is the defect in the title, caught in the wild minutes after the mechanism merged.","files":null}]}